# PUT_OOB carrying a generic SMBus block write.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. The capture is a link coming up and has no
# OOB traffic at all -- it configures the OOB channel at offset 030h and never
# uses it. So nothing here has independent evidence behind it; reading Figure 45
# and section 4.2.3 is the only check there is.
#
# The eSPI header stops after three bytes. Everything from byte 3 on is *data*
# as far as eSPI is concerned -- Figure 45's brace says so and p.73 repeats it in
# prose: "The SMBus Target Address, SMBus Command Opcode, SMBus Byte Count,
# SMBus Data fields and the optional PEC byte are sent as data within the eSPI
# OOB message packet."
#
# What makes that worth decoding rather than dumping is that the packet states
# its own length twice and the difference is load bearing.
#
# --- 1. A block write with the optional PEC byte ---
#
#   06     PUT_OOB                             Table 2, p.26
#   21     Cycle Type: OOB (Tunneled SMBus) Message, 00100001
#          Posted. Note the LSB: bit 0 is 1, so the cycle carries data, which
#          is section 4.1.1's rule on p.46 agreeing with Figure 45.
#   00     Tag 0, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   08     Length[7:0] = 08h -> 8 bytes of data
#   2C     SMBus Target Address 16h in bits [7:1], bit 0 = 0
#          Figure 45 draws that bit as a literal '0' in its own cell, not as
#          part of the address. 2Ch >> 1 is 16h.
#   04     SMBus Command Opcode -- not 0Fh, so not MCTP, and the base spec
#          names no other code
#   04     SMBus Byte Count = 4
#   11 22 33 44   the 4 counted data bytes
#   B7     PEC
#
# The arithmetic p.73 calls "a simple arithmetic operation": Length 8, minus
# the 3 SMBus header bytes, minus a Byte Count of 4, leaves exactly 1 byte, and
# the only thing allowed there is the PEC. Figure 47 works the same sum on the
# page for a 64 byte payload: (3+64+1) = 68.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 06 21 00 08 2C 04 04 11 22 33 44 B7 78
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95

# --- 2. The same message with no PEC byte, and a nonzero Tag ---
#
# One byte shorter, and that one byte is the whole difference. Nothing in the
# packet flags the PEC's absence -- Figure 45 draws it with a dashed border and
# the specification never adds a presence bit. Length 7 = 3 + 4 + 0.
#
#   10     Tag 1, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   07     Length[7:0] = 07h -> 7 bytes
#
# A decoder that assumed a PEC would report 3 data bytes and a PEC of 44h here,
# and a decoder that assumed no PEC would report 5 data bytes above. Both decode
# to the right total length and neither would look wrong.
CMD 06 21 10 07 2C 04 04 11 22 33 44 19
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95
